title: 'Decided: whether a perspective draws the buffer otherwise is judged by drawing it; a press draws only the candidates up to the next one, and sends the drawing it chose.'
pid: 73328c83-107a-4993-b687-bf40fa8ed653
body: |2-
  The first build estimated it from the positions where substitution
  acts, and the review found two ways that went wrong: a switch could
  hide the very nodes that put a later author in the set, so "next"
  skipped that author for good; and an author could join who changed
  nothing, beneath a node shown raw. So each candidate's drawing is
  compared, as text, with the root authors' ('view_drawn_from'). The
  candidates are the user and the owners of the overriders of what
  either the root authors' drawing or the buffer's own draws, so every
  author found from the default is found from anywhere. Not found: an
  author whose overrides apply only beneath a node another perspective
  puts in place, until that perspective is shown.
  Until 2026-11-14 a press drew the whole cycle (the root authors' and
  every candidate) and then drew the chosen one again as the rerender:
  2 + N drawings, one reaching the buffer. Jeff called that silly. Now
  "next" tries candidates in rank order from the one after the current
  perspective and stops at the first that changes the view
  ('first_ranked_after'), and the drawing that chose it is the rerender
  sent ('PreparedRerenders::of_one_content_view'); a named owner draws
  only itself. So a press draws the root authors' view plus each owner
  tried: usually two drawings, one when wrapping back. The cycle's
  members, order and refusals did not change. Measured on a release
  build: under 1 ms of server CPU per press on the test fixture's view
  of R (3 candidates), 54 ms falling to 20 ms on a synthetic
  992-headline view with 4 candidates (about 9 ms a drawing). Still
  undone, and the next saving if a press is ever slow: the root authors'
  drawing is needed only as the baseline every comparison is made
  against, and could be cached between presses while the graph's
  generation is unchanged.
